naps":64,"epa_per_play":0.05937,"pass_rate":0.6719,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5909,"def_epa_per_play":0.07166},{"week":17,"opponent":"DAL","home":false,"pf":25.0,"pa":22.0,"off_snaps":70,"epa_per_play":0.1625,"pass_rate":0.5857,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6744,"def_epa_per_play":0.08635},{"week":18,"opponent":"SEA","home":true,"pf":30.0,"pa":38.0,"off_snaps":72,"epa_per_play":-0.02286,"pass_rate":0.6111,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5593,"def_epa_per_play":0.1818}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2023,"team":"PHI","distance":1.05,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CLE","distance":1.13,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"CHI","distance":1.151,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Matt Nagy"},{"season":2023,"team":"BUF","distance":1.171,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PHI","distance":1.194,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"}],"defense":[{"season":2025,"team":"TB","distance":0.4073,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"},{"season":2022,"team":"GB","distance":0.6022,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"TB","distance":0.657,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"},{"season":2021,"team":"TB","distance":0.6681,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"},{"season":2022,"team":"TB","distance":0.7229,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Cardinals went 11-6 in 2021 and outscored opponents 449 to 366. Both sides of the ball were strong, with the offense 9th in EPA per play and the defense 6th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 10 personnel usage and the most linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Cardinals offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the highest 10 personnel usage and the highest no-huddle rate in the league. Kyler Murray ran the passing game, with Christian Kirk leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Cardinals defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 22nd. Markus Golden led the team with 11 sacks, and Chandler Jones added 10.5, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Schematically, they had the most linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Cardinals were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 81% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 16% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Kyler Murray played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,787 yards. Targets were spread around, with Christian Kirk and A.J. Green leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by James Conner and Chase Edmonds. On defense, Markus Golden led the team with 11 sacks and Byron Murphy picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Kliff Kingsbury called the offense as head coach and Vance Joseph called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Eagles and the 2024 Browns. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Buccaneers under Todd Bowles and the 2022 Packers.","trends":"The offense went from 14th in EPA per play in 2020 to 9th in 2021, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 11th in EPA allowed per play in 2020 to 6th in 2021, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was Cover 1 rate, which went from 36% to 15%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2021. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}